From: MedyMa <aneylovemzy@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 23 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 6.6] net: sfp: force module re-init on warm reboot via TX_DISABLE

On warm reboot the kernel restarts but SFP modules stay powered.
Their internal state machine may be stuck (especially I2C interface or
PHY negotiation state), and the module never responds to the standard
probe sequence.  The BPI-R4 I2C mux idle-disconnect handles the bus
side, but the module itself needs a clean reset.

Detect warm reboot by checking mod-def0 during sfp_probe(): if a module
is already physically present at probe time (mod-def0 active), we are
in a warm-boot scenario.  Briefly assert TX_DISABLE (100 ms) to force
the module through a complete hardware reset, then wait 100 ms for the
module to stabilise before proceeding with normal probing.

Cold boot is unaffected because during a genuine power-cycle the module
takes several hundred milliseconds to become ready, and mod-def0 will
not yet be asserted by the time sfp_probe() runs.

Signed-off-by: MedyMa <aneylovemzy@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/phy/sfp.c | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c
@@ -3046,6 +3046,22 @@ static int sfp_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		sfp->gpio_irq[i] = gpiod_to_irq(sfp->gpio[i]);
 	}
 
+	/* Warm boot: pulse TX_DISABLE to reset a module that stayed
+	 * powered across reboot, as its I2C state machine may be stuck.
+	 * GPIO0=mod-def0, GPIO3=tx-disable.
+	 */
+	if (sfp->gpio[0] && !IS_ERR(sfp->gpio[0]) &&
+	    gpiod_get_value_cansleep(sfp->gpio[0])) {
+		if (sfp->gpio[3] && !IS_ERR(sfp->gpio[3])) {
+			dev_dbg(&pdev->dev,
+				"warm boot: resetting module via TX_DISABLE\n");
+			gpiod_set_value_cansleep(sfp->gpio[3], 1);
+			msleep(100);
+			gpiod_set_value_cansleep(sfp->gpio[3], 0);
+			msleep(100);
+		}
+	}
+
 	/* There is no use for SFPs with no i2c bus */
 	if (!sfp->i2c)
 		return 0;
